# Ch 2 - Sliding Windows and Embeddings

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:gpt3_tokenizer, "~> 0.1.1"},
  {:nx, "~> 0.7.0"},
  {:req, "~> 0.5.17"}
])
```

## About this Notebook

A small, learning-oriented word and positional embedding notebook written in Elixir while working through Chapter 2 of *Build a Large Language Model (from Scratch)*. The focus is on clarity and understanding rather than completeness or performance. More context is available at [https://latentwords.com](https://www.latentwords.com/from-tokens-to-training-data-building-sliding-window-embeddings-in-elixir/)

## Initialize Embeddings

Create random embedding matrices for tokens and for token positions.

```elixir
defmodule Embeddings do
  defstruct scale: 0.02,
            seed: 0
  
  # Use default scale and seed when no config is provided
  def init_embedding(shape) do
    init_embedding(shape, %__MODULE__{})
  end
  
  # Initialize an embedding matrix of the given shape with small random values
  def init_embedding({rows, cols}, %__MODULE__{} = config) do
    {emb, _} =
      Nx.Random.uniform(
        Nx.Random.key(config.seed),
        0.0,
        1.0,
        shape: {rows, cols},
        type: {:f, 32}
      )

    Nx.multiply(emb, config.scale)
  end
end
```

## Load tokens and initialize embeddings

First, we read the document, tokenize the text, and then initialize the embeddings.

```elixir
text = Req.get!("https://raw.githubusercontent.com/mcargian/llmfromscratch/main/files/the-verdict.txt").body
tokens = Gpt3Tokenizer.encode(text)

vocab_size = Enum.max(tokens) + 1
embedding_dim = 8
max_length = 4
token_embedding = Embeddings.init_embedding({vocab_size, embedding_dim})
pos_embedding   = Embeddings.init_embedding({max_length, embedding_dim})
:ok
```

## Sliding Window and Apply Embeddings

This module creates sliding windows of token IDs, batches them into tensors, and then replaces input token IDs with token and positional embeddings.

```elixir
defmodule SlidingWindow do
  defstruct stride: 1,
            batch_size: 1,
            max_length: 4
  
  # Stream the tokens for batch training
  def stream_tokens(tokens, opts) when is_list(opts) do
     stream_tokens(tokens, struct!(__MODULE__, opts))
  end

  def stream_tokens(tokens, %__MODULE__{} = config) do
     tokens
     |> Stream.chunk_every(config.max_length + 1, config.stride, :discard)
     |> Stream.map(&create_input_target_pair(&1, config.max_length))
     |> Stream.chunk_every(config.batch_size, config.batch_size, :discard)
     |> Stream.map(&batch_to_tensors/1)
  end

  # Create input and target ID pairs from a window of tokens
  defp create_input_target_pair(window, max_length) do
    input = Enum.take(window, max_length)
    target = window |> tl() |> Enum.take(max_length)
    {input, target}
  end

  # Convert a batch of input and target ID pairs to tensors
  defp batch_to_tensors(batch) do
    {xs, ys} = Enum.unzip(batch)
    {Nx.tensor(xs, type: {:s, 64}), Nx.tensor(ys, type: {:s, 64})}
  end

  # Replace input token IDs with their embedding vectors; targets remain token IDs
  def embed_batch({xs, ys}, token_embedding, pos_embedding) do
    xs_embedded = Nx.take(token_embedding, xs)
    xs_with_pos = Nx.add(xs_embedded, pos_embedding)
    {xs_with_pos, ys}
  end

  # Lazily apply embedding lookup to the inputs of each batch in the stream
  def embed_stream(stream, token_embedding, pos_embedding) do
    Stream.map(stream, &embed_batch(&1, token_embedding, pos_embedding))
  end

end
```

## From Tokens to Training Data

Finally, we create a stream that produces embedded inputs and target token IDs. The input embeddings include positional embeddings so the model can learn how position affects each token. Calling `Enum.take(1)` forces evaluation of the stream and returns the first batch, which contains the input embeddings (with positional information) and the target token IDs.

```elixir
stream =
  tokens
  |> SlidingWindow.stream_tokens(batch_size: 1, max_length: max_length)
  |> SlidingWindow.embed_stream(token_embedding, pos_embedding)
  |> Enum.take(1)
```
